-- §21 Which corner of the path's factor each bound of a carried remainder took, and whether it mattered.
# One row per settled node a remainder walk carries up, with the sign of the operand at each bound
# and whether taking corners gave a different figure from multiplying bound by bound.
#
# The conversion of a signed quantity is two slopes and a sign
#   A demand, a nameplate and a draw cannot be negative, so the smaller factor always lands on the
#   smaller figure and `composition/converted.sqlc` multiplies bound by bound. A remainder is a
#   difference and may be negative, so `composition/settled_remainders.sqlc` takes the least and the
#   greatest over both corners instead: more of a shortfall converted at a larger rate is the smaller
#   number. Those are the two branches of one operator, and this section says which branch each
#   filing actually reached.
#
# `corner_rule_bites` is a conjunction, and that is the point
#   It needs a factor with width AND a negative operand at that bound. Either alone changes nothing.
#   Read the counts here rather than assuming: if both populations are sizeable and the column is
#   false throughout, the two do not overlap in this corpus, and the least and the greatest are
#   standing ready for a document nobody has filed.
#
# An empty conjunction is a fact about the evidence
#   It is not a reason to simplify the arithmetic. A negative remainder is a layer whose demand
#   exceeds its supply, which is the interference case the model exists to measure, and a factor with
#   width is ordinary. The identity is proved against a constructed operand in
#   `src/proofs/README.md`, entries `positive_factor_componentwise` and `two_slopes`, precisely
#   because no filing reaches it.
-- composition/conversion_slopes.sqlc, ordered so any biting row reads first.
SELECT c.composition                AS "composition!",
       c.composed_layer             AS "composed_layer!",
       c.node_filing                AS "node_filing!",
       c.node_layer                 AS "node_layer!",
       c.depth                      AS "depth!",
       c.spread_factor              AS "spread_factor!",
       c.operand_low::float8        AS operand_low,
       c.operand_high::float8       AS operand_high,
       c.low_took                   AS "low_took!",
       c.high_took                  AS "high_took!",
       c.corner_rule_bites          AS corner_rule_bites
FROM (
    :compose(composition/conversion_slopes.sqlc)
) c
ORDER BY c.corner_rule_bites DESC NULLS LAST, c.spread_factor DESC, 1, 2, 4
